WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.437
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.038 --> 00:00:09.080
RETO MEIER: Welcome back
to the "Next" live show.

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:11.750
As promised, fellow developer
advocate Kelsey Hightower

00:00:11.750 --> 00:00:12.910
is here to join us.

00:00:12.910 --> 00:00:14.160
Thanks for being here, Kelsey.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:15.540
KELSEY HIGHTOWER:
I'm happy to be here.

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:17.582
STEPHANIE WONG: So earlier
we heard TK's keynote.

00:00:17.582 --> 00:00:19.560
What are you most excited about?

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:21.977
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: Well, given
my background in Kubernetes,

00:00:21.977 --> 00:00:25.070
I'm most excited
about Anthos, which

00:00:25.070 --> 00:00:27.110
is kind of giving
Kubernetes to more

00:00:27.110 --> 00:00:29.688
people and their environment,
so they can run it on-prem,

00:00:29.688 --> 00:00:30.980
you can run it on Google Cloud.

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:32.299
Also I like the
addition of Istio,

00:00:32.299 --> 00:00:34.970
so giving people the ability to
manage their networking playing

00:00:34.970 --> 00:00:36.560
like they do for their compute.

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:38.240
And I think the
other thing I'm most

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:40.380
happy about is more
managed databases,

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:42.020
so now having SQL server.

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:44.060
Lots of our customers
ask for SQL server,

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:46.655
so pairing managed databases
with something like Kubernetes

00:00:46.655 --> 00:00:47.780
is kind of a perfect match.

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:48.230
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:00:48.230 --> 00:00:49.897
And Google has a
really strong tradition

00:00:49.897 --> 00:00:52.970
of developer teams passing on
creative software innovations

00:00:52.970 --> 00:00:55.430
to our emerging
developer audiences.

00:00:55.430 --> 00:00:57.530
What tools can you tell
us about that are also

00:00:57.530 --> 00:00:59.810
empowering our enterprise
developers with years

00:00:59.810 --> 00:01:01.560
of experience and
proprietary systems?

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:02.810
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: Yeah, I
think for the biggest thing

00:01:02.810 --> 00:01:05.420
is meeting developers where
they are, so kind of integrating

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:07.633
with their IDEs, giving
them familiar open source

00:01:07.633 --> 00:01:08.175
technologies.

00:01:08.175 --> 00:01:09.770
So a of developers
are running things

00:01:09.770 --> 00:01:12.380
like Docker and Kubernetes
on their laptop--

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:13.670
Envoy packaged in Istio.

00:01:13.670 --> 00:01:15.920
So what we're doing is taking
all of these open source

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:18.440
projects that you may be
using on your own machines,

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:20.053
maybe even internally,
and we're just

00:01:20.053 --> 00:01:21.470
adding a lot of
that support stuff

00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:24.270
that you would need to run it
in production in the enterprise.

00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:26.478
RETO MEIER: So we can see
that values are shifting up

00:01:26.478 --> 00:01:28.340
the stack with higher
level sets of services

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:29.900
and new abstractions.

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:32.222
How is automation evolving
the developer experience?

00:01:32.222 --> 00:01:33.680
KELSEY HIGHTOWER:
Previously, we've

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:36.560
been trying to automate a
lot of bad practices, right?

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:39.650
If you automate directly on the
virtual machine, for example,

00:01:39.650 --> 00:01:41.720
now you're starting to
automate around scripts,

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:43.678
we had a lot of configuration
management tools,

00:01:43.678 --> 00:01:45.030
but the APIs weren't there.

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:47.000
Now, as you move up the
stack, things like Kubernetes

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:49.583
give us a declarative interface
for deploying on a distributed

00:01:49.583 --> 00:01:50.330
system--

00:01:50.330 --> 00:01:52.690
so containers, batch
drops, con jobs.

00:01:52.690 --> 00:01:54.440
So now we start to
think about automation.

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:56.635
You're finally targeting APIs.

00:01:56.635 --> 00:01:58.760
So what we were able to do
for traditional software

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:01.253
development, we're now able
to do for infrastructure.

00:02:01.253 --> 00:02:03.170
So bringing things like
Istio, which gives you

00:02:03.170 --> 00:02:05.210
a declarative API
for the networking,

00:02:05.210 --> 00:02:08.120
things like our service add
on give you a declarative API

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:09.240
for your serverless stack.

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:10.740
So when you look
across the spectrum

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:13.280
now for infrastructure, you're
now programming, finally,

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:14.363
towards well-defined APIs.

00:02:14.363 --> 00:02:16.530
STEPHANIE WONG: So this is
sort of supporting people

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:17.360
where they are.

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:20.120
And I want to talk about GKE
on-prem and hybrid workloads.

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:21.620
What are some of
the benefits of it,

00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:23.745
and what can sys admins
kind of reap from that?

00:02:23.745 --> 00:02:26.120
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: Yeah, I
think since Kubernetes came out

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:28.010
everyone was like,
I want it, right?

00:02:28.010 --> 00:02:30.410
And GKE, when you
click that button,

00:02:30.410 --> 00:02:31.920
you just get this
magical cluster--

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:33.685
production ready, ready to go.

00:02:33.685 --> 00:02:35.060
And the next thing
is, well, what

00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:36.718
about my existing environment?

00:02:36.718 --> 00:02:38.510
And many people have
kind of tried to do it

00:02:38.510 --> 00:02:41.380
and not everyone has the same
level of expertise or time.

00:02:41.380 --> 00:02:43.130
So the nice thing about
this is that we're

00:02:43.130 --> 00:02:47.660
going to take GKE, the same bits
that we used to host on GCP,

00:02:47.660 --> 00:02:49.730
we're now bringing that
into your data center.

00:02:49.730 --> 00:02:52.022
So we've given people clear
guidance on the lower level

00:02:52.022 --> 00:02:54.350
requirements, and then we
just layered GKE on top,

00:02:54.350 --> 00:02:57.470
and then we do the same
APIs, the same control plans.

00:02:57.470 --> 00:03:00.110
So if you're using things
like Terraform on GCP,

00:03:00.110 --> 00:03:02.383
you're now able to
point that same stack

00:03:02.383 --> 00:03:04.550
in your on-prem environment--
get that same workflow

00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:06.000
that you love so much on GKE.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:06.708
RETO MEIER: Nice.

00:03:06.708 --> 00:03:09.230
Now, Kubernetes is being
adopted and adapted

00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:11.420
to meet more and
more technical needs.

00:03:11.420 --> 00:03:14.930
Tell us about how GKE hub helps
admins with hybrid environments

00:03:14.930 --> 00:03:16.130
do their jobs better.

00:03:16.130 --> 00:03:17.220
KELSEY HIGHTOWER:
Yeah, so GKE hub--

00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:19.160
so once you kind of get
a taste of Kubernetes,

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:21.493
the next thing is you want
Kubernetes everywhere, right?

00:03:21.493 --> 00:03:24.650
Every problem you see, you want
to rub Kubernetes on top of it.

00:03:24.650 --> 00:03:27.110
So a lot of our customers
have more than one cluster.

00:03:27.110 --> 00:03:28.610
If you think about it,
a lot of our customers

00:03:28.610 --> 00:03:30.680
value high availability,
they'll have a cluster

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:31.975
per region or per zone.

00:03:31.975 --> 00:03:34.100
So once you start to have
a collection of clusters,

00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:36.230
even you bring in
on-prem into the mix,

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:38.600
you need a place to aggregate
all of those things.

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:40.370
So a GKE hub represents
is the ability

00:03:40.370 --> 00:03:42.680
to aggregate all of your
Kubernetes clusters, even

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:44.902
the ones on-prem, and
then once you have that,

00:03:44.902 --> 00:03:46.610
you have a centralized
place to do things

00:03:46.610 --> 00:03:48.177
like policy management.

00:03:48.177 --> 00:03:49.760
So once you have a
Kubernetes cluster,

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:50.670
the thing you have
to think about

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:52.380
is security across
all those clusters.

00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:54.380
And one thing that's kind
of hard or error prone

00:03:54.380 --> 00:03:55.880
is you have to try
to figure out how

00:03:55.880 --> 00:03:57.870
to get your configuration
correct across all

00:03:57.870 --> 00:03:58.950
of those clusters.

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:00.950
So the hub is going to
be the anchor point where

00:04:00.950 --> 00:04:03.153
we build a lot of those
additional tools that

00:04:03.153 --> 00:04:05.320
allow you to manage everything
from a central place.

00:04:05.320 --> 00:04:06.075
RETO MEIER: Nice.

00:04:06.075 --> 00:04:06.950
Well, thanks, Kelsey.

00:04:06.950 --> 00:04:08.780
We really appreciate
you taking the time.

00:04:08.780 --> 00:04:10.322
KELSEY HIGHTOWER:
Awesome, thank you.

00:04:10.322 --> 00:04:13.160
[MUSIC PLAYING]

